\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {4}{\ignorespaces Two throats at rest: the sign rule (a,b) and the force law (c) on the far-side-matched pairs (Sec.~\ref {sec:model:solve}), and the placement calibration of the superposed campaign (d,e). (a)~Separation change of two matched $d=12$ pairs one sign apart: like-signed ($\sigma =+1$, dashed) opens, opposite-signed ($\sigma =-1$, solid) closes. Separations are inverse-$\chi $-weighted centroids of the midplane $\chi $ pits. (b)~Pull-to-push ratio against the prediction $(Q{+}1)/(Q{-}1)=3/2$ (gold), where $Q=|F_\phi /F_{\rm  grav}|=(a^2+m^2)/m^2=5$ is the scalar-to-gravity force ratio of Eq.~\eqref  {eq:force}: measured $1.462\pm 0.022$ (scatter over $15$ slices); grey, the gauge-settling window $t<3.5$, excluded; open circles past $t=10.5$, where the closing pair has eaten a tenth of its gap and the displacements are no longer small, excluded too. (c)~The force law on the matched ladder: how far like-signed pairs released from rest at $d=12/14/16/18$ have pushed themselves apart by $t=11.5$ (dots), against inverse-square in the coordinate separation scaled through $d=12$ (grey, dashed) and $A/(d+\delta )^2$ fitted on $d=12$--$16$ alone (gold), dotted where it predicts the $d=18$ rung (Sec.~\ref {sec:pair:force}). If the push were inverse-square in $d$, every dot would lie on the grey curve; the dots rise above it, and the gold fit absorbs the excess with the offset $\delta $. (d)~Per-mouth areal radius of freshly placed pairs at $t=0$ (circles), exceeding the isolated $R_\star $ (dotted) by an excess $\propto d^{-1.16}$. Gold squares: the head-on scout's mouths at the separation reached; open grey squares (here and in e) lie closer than the closest probe. (e)~The scout against the placement curve, $\delta R/R=R_{\rm  scout}/R_{\rm  place}-1$: the $-0.13\%$ plateau to $t=5$ calibrates the tracker's grid snap, and the squeeze then grows to $-4.0\%$ by $t=13$. In the shaded band the curve is held at its last point, so the magnitude is a lower bound; a boost can only raise a coordinate-sphere reading, so a reading below the curve is a genuine squeeze.}}{11}{figure.4}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:pair}{{4}{11}{Two throats at rest: the sign rule (a,b) and the force law (c) on the far-side-matched pairs (Sec.~\ref {sec:model:solve}), and the placement calibration of the superposed campaign (d,e). (a)~Separation change of two matched $d=\clmPairSeparation $ pairs one sign apart: like-signed ($\sigma =+1$, dashed) opens, opposite-signed ($\sigma =-1$, solid) closes. Separations are inverse-$\chi $-weighted centroids of the midplane $\chi $ pits. (b)~Pull-to-push ratio against the prediction $(Q{+}1)/(Q{-}1)=\clmScalarQRatio $ (gold), where $Q=|F_\phi /F_{\rm grav}|=(a^2+m^2)/m^2=\clmScalarQ $ is the scalar-to-gravity force ratio of Eq.~\eqref {eq:force}: measured $\clmMatchedSignRatio \pm \clmMatchedSignRatioErr $ (scatter over $15$ slices); grey, the gauge-settling window $t<\clmSignSettleWindow $, excluded; open circles past $t=\clmSignWindowEnd $, where the closing pair has eaten a tenth of its gap and the displacements are no longer small, excluded too. (c)~The force law on the matched ladder: how far like-signed pairs released from rest at $d=12/14/16/18$ have pushed themselves apart by $t=11.5$ (dots), against inverse-square in the coordinate separation scaled through $d=12$ (grey, dashed) and $A/(d+\delta )^2$ fitted on $d=12$--$16$ alone (gold), dotted where it predicts the $d=18$ rung (Sec.~\ref {sec:pair:force}). If the push were inverse-square in $d$, every dot would lie on the grey curve; the dots rise above it, and the gold fit absorbs the excess with the offset $\delta $. (d)~Per-mouth areal radius of freshly placed pairs at $t=0$ (circles), exceeding the isolated $R_\star $ (dotted) by an excess $\propto d^{-\clmPlacementSlope }$. Gold squares: the head-on scout's mouths at the separation reached; open grey squares (here and in e) lie closer than the closest probe. (e)~The scout against the placement curve, $\delta R/R=R_{\rm scout}/R_{\rm place}-1$: the $\clmScoutPlateau \%$ plateau to $t=\clmScoutPlateauEnd $ calibrates the tracker's grid snap, and the squeeze then grows to $\clmScoutSqueeze \%$ by $t=\clmScoutLastInRange $. In the shaded band the curve is held at its last point, so the magnitude is a lower bound; a boost can only raise a coordinate-sphere reading, so a reading below the curve is a genuine squeeze}{figure.4}{}}
