\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {7}{\ignorespaces The waveform gallery: one row per wormhole scenario on one retarded clock. Left: $r\,\mathrm  {Re}\,\Psi _4$ of the dominant mode at the innermost sphere, each row on its own scale; row (a) is the $\varepsilon _2=0.05$ arm, the only one loud and long enough to carry the ringdown, with the damped-sinusoid ringdown fit of Sec.~\ref {sec:single:gw} dashed (period $20.6\,M$). Caps mark where the drawn record ends: the collapsing throat where its numerical floor, the spherical control's own $r\Psi _4^{2,0}$, passes $10\%$ of the burst peak; the spiral, carried from $t=57$ by the frozen-core arm, at that core's light cone $t=57+(R-1.9)$; the fly-by, complete to $t=100$, at $t-R=50$ on every sphere (Sec.~\ref {sec:gw:flyby}); the head-on, drawn from its production chain's glued record (the caption of Fig.~\ref {fig:headon_collapse}), where the late-time noise of its level-1 refinement cube (from $t=80$ at $R\le 20$; level-0 spheres run to the end) reaches each sphere. Grey in (c) and (d): the vacuum BBH spiral and fly-by, black holes with the same $d$ and $p$ (Sec.~\ref {sec:gw}), at the same sphere and on the same scale. The vacuum spiral's merger comes later and lower than the drainhole burst (Sec.~\ref {sec:controls:bbh}); the vacuum fly-by, drawn over the fly-by's window, radiates one cycle as the holes swing away from periapsis and nothing at the pass, where the fly-by radiates its arch. Right: the amplitude envelope at every sphere on its own retarded time (ink to faint, inner to outer): $|r\Psi _4|$ for the complex $(2,2)$ modes, the analytic-signal envelope for the real $(2,0)$ modes, whose $|r\Psi _4|$ would fall to zero at each node. A signal moving at $c$ and falling as $1/r$ folds onto one curve; each envelope rises with its burst (collapse, plunge, pass) and stops before a contaminant grows into it. Gold dots: the correlated wavefront. The quoted $v/c$ is the lag of the whole complex waveform between neighbouring spheres over the drawn records, which, unlike peak timing, survives flat-topped envelopes (Sec.~\ref {sec:controls:bbh}). Speeds: throat $0.94/0.97/0.95$, head-on $0.94/0.88/0.95$, spiral $1.00/1.00/1.00$, fly-by $1.00/1.00/1.00$.}}{17}{figure.7}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:gw_gallery}{{7}{17}{The waveform gallery: one row per wormhole scenario on one retarded clock. Left: $r\,\mathrm {Re}\,\Psi _4$ of the dominant mode at the innermost sphere, each row on its own scale; row (a) is the $\varepsilon _2=\clmGwSeedStrong $ arm, the only one loud and long enough to carry the ringdown, with the damped-sinusoid ringdown fit of Sec.~\ref {sec:single:gw} dashed (period $\clmGwThroatPeriod \,M$). Caps mark where the drawn record ends: the collapsing throat where its numerical floor, the spherical control's own $r\Psi _4^{2,0}$, passes $\clmGwDrawFloorFrac \%$ of the burst peak; the spiral, carried from $t=\clmGwFreezeStart $ by the frozen-core arm, at that core's light cone $t=\clmGwFreezeStart +(R-\clmGwFillSkin )$; the fly-by, complete to $t=\clmGwFlybyEnd $, at $t-R=\clmGwDrawFlybyRetarded $ on every sphere (Sec.~\ref {sec:gw:flyby}); the head-on, drawn from its production chain's glued record (the caption of Fig.~\ref {fig:headon_collapse}), where the late-time noise of its level-1 refinement cube (from $t=\clmGwHeadonNoiseGate $ at $R\le 20$; level-0 spheres run to the end) reaches each sphere. Grey in (c) and (d): the vacuum BBH spiral and fly-by, black holes with the same $d$ and $p$ (Sec.~\ref {sec:gw}), at the same sphere and on the same scale. The vacuum spiral's merger comes later and lower than the drainhole burst (Sec.~\ref {sec:controls:bbh}); the vacuum fly-by, drawn over the fly-by's window, radiates one cycle as the holes swing away from periapsis and nothing at the pass, where the fly-by radiates its arch. Right: the amplitude envelope at every sphere on its own retarded time (ink to faint, inner to outer): $|r\Psi _4|$ for the complex $(2,2)$ modes, the analytic-signal envelope for the real $(2,0)$ modes, whose $|r\Psi _4|$ would fall to zero at each node. A signal moving at $c$ and falling as $1/r$ folds onto one curve; each envelope rises with its burst (collapse, plunge, pass) and stops before a contaminant grows into it. Gold dots: the correlated wavefront. The quoted $v/c$ is the lag of the whole complex waveform between neighbouring spheres over the drawn records, which, unlike peak timing, survives flat-topped envelopes (Sec.~\ref {sec:controls:bbh}). Speeds: throat $\clmGwSpeedThroatA /\clmGwSpeedThroatB /\clmGwSpeedThroatC $, head-on $\clmGwSpeedHeadonA /\clmGwSpeedHeadonB /\clmGwSpeedHeadonC $, spiral $\clmGwSpeedSpiralA /\clmGwSpeedSpiralB /\clmGwSpeedSpiralC $, fly-by $\clmGwSpeedFlybyA /\clmGwSpeedFlybyB /\clmGwSpeedFlybyC $}{figure.7}{}}
